     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change the safety problem. 50 mg is five times the maximum daily dose, and I have no clinical reason to think it's intended. Because of amlodipine's long half-life, an overdose could cause profound, prolonged hypotension and shock. The harm would also be hard to reverse once the patient had taken the drug.\n\nAlso, the order needs to come from a licensed prescriber who is treating this patient. A confirmation from a non-clinician can't take the place of that.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a standard dose now.** I can enter amlodipine 5 mg or 10 mg by mouth once daily, if the treating prescriber tells me which.\n- **Take a prescriber's order.** If a clinician has a specific reason for a dose above the labeled maximum, they can enter it or tell me directly. Pharmacy should review it as well.\n- **Pass the concern along.** If the order was meant to be 5 mg, the prescriber can correct it quickly.\n\nTell me which of these you'd like."
      }
     ],
